<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8" ?>

<section xml:id="section-MVTplus" >
<title>Interpreting Derivatives</title>

<introduction>
	<p>In addition to finding extrema derivatives have additional properties that are easily illustrated with geometry. It is helpful for these to think of tangents as vectors rather than lines.</p>
</introduction>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-Rolles" >
	<title>Rolle's Theorem</title>

	<activity xml:id="activity-rolles-theorem">
		<title>Rolle's Theorem</title>
		<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to discover a property of tangents of sufficiently nice curves.</p></introduction>
		<task><p>Sketch a curve per these directions. <ol>
			<li>Draw a point on the left of the paper.</li>
			<li>Draw a point on the right of the paper at the same height.</li>
			<li>Draw the line connecting these two points.</li>
			<li>Draw a curve from the left point to the right point that does not cross the line.</li>
		</ol></p></task>
		<task><p>Experiment with the tangents. <ol>
			<li>Place a pencil or similar object on the left most point.</li>
			<li>Rotate it so that it is tangent to the curve at that point.</li>
			<li>Note the direction of the tangent vector at this point.</li>
			<li>Drag the tangent vector across the curve. Keep it tangent as you drag it.</li>
			<li>Note the direction of the tangent vector at the right endpoint.</li>
			<li>Contrast the direction of the tangent at the left and right.</li>
		</ol></p></task>
		<task><p>The contrast in the previous task guarantees that another slope occurs as shown next. <ol>
			<li>What kind of line is the line connecting the two points?</li>
			<li>Drag the tangent vector across the curve again.</li>
			<li>Does it ever match the line connecting the endpoints?</li>
			<li>Is this (matching or not matching) always the case?</li>
		</ol></p></task>
	</activity>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-MVT" >
	<title>Mean Value Theorem</title>

	<activity xml:id="activity-mvt-theorem">
		<title>Mean Value Theorem</title>
		<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to discover a property of tangents of sufficiently nice curves.</p></introduction>
		<task><p>Draw a curve as described. <ol>
			<li>Draw a point on the left of the paper.</li>
			<li>Draw a point on the right of the paper at a different height. (Hint: you can use your Rolle's Theorem example if you are clever.)</li>
			<li>Draw a curve from the left point to the right point.</li>
		</ol></p></task>
		<task><p>Experiment with the tangents again. <ol>
			<li>Place a pencil or similar object on the left most point.</li>
			<li>Rotate it so that it is tangent to the curve at that point.</li>
			<li>Drag the tangent vector across the curve. Keep it tangent as you drag it.</li>
			<li>Draw a line connecting the starting and ending points.</li>
			<li>Drag the tangent vector across the curve again.</li>
			<li>Does it ever match the line connecting the endpoints?</li>
			<li>Would this (matching or not matching) always be the case for this kind of curve?</li>
		</ol></p></task>
	</activity>
	
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-increasing" >
	<title>Increasing and Decreasing Intervals</title>

	<definition xml:id="definition-increasing">
		<title>Increasing</title>
		<statement><p>A function is <term>increasing</term> on an interval if and only if <m>f(x_2) \ge f(x_1)</m> whenever <m>x_2 > x_1</m>.</p></statement>
	</definition>

	<definition xml:id="definition-decreasing">
		<title>Decreasing</title>
		<statement><p>A function is <term>decreasing</term> on an interval if and only if <m>f(x_2) \le f(x_1)</m> whenever <m>x_2 > x_1</m>.</p></statement>
	</definition>

	<definition xml:id="definition-strictly-increasing">
		<title>Strictly Increasing</title>
		<statement><p>A function is <term>strictly increasing</term> on an interval if and only if <m>f(x_2) > f(x_1)</m> whenever <m>x_2 > x_1</m>.</p></statement>
	</definition>

	<exercise><statement><p>Find the difference between <term>increasing</term> and <term>strictly increasing</term>.</p></statement></exercise>

	<activity xml:id="activity-increasing-interval">
		<title>Increasing Intervals</title>
		<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to discover a property of increasing segments on sufficiently nice curves.</p></introduction>
		<task><p>Sketch a segment of a curve that is increasing.</p></task>
		<task><p>Sketch tangent vectors on this segment.</p></task>
		<task><p>What direction(s) do they face? Be general.</p></task>
		<task><p>The slope of the tangent is computed using what calculus concept?</p></task>
		<task><p>What can you say about the slope of the tangent (the number) on increasing segments?</p></task>
		<task><p>Conjecture a test to determine on what intervals a function is increasing.</p></task>
	</activity>
	
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-first-derivative-test">
	<title>1st Derivative Test</title>

	<activity xml:id="activity-first-derivative-test">
		<title>Discovering the 1st Derivative Test</title>
		<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to notice how increasing and decreasing segments are connected to relative extrema.</p></introduction>
		<task><p>Sketch a graph of a function with at least three relative extrema.</p></task>
		<task><p>Identify and label increasing and decreasing segments.</p></task>
		<task><p>Identify and label relative extrema.</p></task>
		<task><p>Based on these examples, conjecture a means to find relative extrema.</p></task>
		<task><p>How can you tell the difference between maxima and minima using this test?</p></task>
	</activity>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-concavity">
	<title>Concavity</title>

	<activity xml:id="activity-discover-concavity">
		<title>Discovering the Meaning of a Second Derivative</title>
		<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to notice how tangent vectors change from left to right on an increasing or on a decreasing segment.</p></introduction>
		<task><p>For each curve in <xref ref="figure-concave-segments-images" /> do the following.
		<ol>
			<li>Label the segment as increasing or decreasing.</li>
			<li>Based on these examples can a detailed graph be drawn knowing only if segments are increasing or decreasing?</li>
		</ol></p></task>
		<task><p>For each segment in <xref ref="figure-concave-segments-images" /> answer the following questions.</p>
		<p><ol>
				<li>What direction are all the tangent vector pointing? Your answer should be like <q>up left</q> or <q>down right</q>. Does the direction change on this segment?</li>
				<li>Consider the tangent vector on the left and right of this segment. Which (tangent) slope is greater? Note a slope of +1 and a slope of -1 are equally steep just facing different directions. 2 is a steeper slope than 1. -3 is a steeper slope than -1/3.</li>
				<li>Based on the previous answer, how are the slopes of the tangent vectors changing from left to right?</li>
		</ol></p></task>
		<task><p>The last question above asked about how something was <q>changing</q>. What calculus concept is this using?</p></task>

	</activity>

	<figure xml:id="figure-concave-segments-images">
		<caption>Illustrations of Concavity</caption>

		<sbsgroup>
		<sidebyside widths="35%, 35%">
			<image source="images/concave_ex1" />
			<image source="images/concave_ex2" />
		</sidebyside>
		<sidebyside widths="35%, 35%">
			<image source="images/concave_ex3" />
			<image source="images/concave_ex4" />
		</sidebyside>
		</sbsgroup>
	</figure>

</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="subsection-second-derivative-test">
	<title>2nd Derivative Test</title>

	<activity xml:id="activity-second-derivative-test">
		<title>Discovering the 2nd Derivative Test</title>
		<introduction><p>The goal of this activity is to notice how concave up and concave down segments are connected to relative extrema.</p><p>In <xref ref="figure-concave-segments-images" /> the top left is increasing concave down, the top right is decreasing concave down, bottom left is decreasing concave up, and the bottom right is increasing concave up.</p></introduction>
		<task>
			<p>Concavity and Relative Maxima
			<ol>
			<li>Draw a curve with a relative maximum. The second derivative should exist at the maximum.</li>
			<li>What is the concavity at the maximum?</li>
			</ol></p>
		</task>
		<task><p>Concavity and Relative Minima
			<ol>
			<li>Draw a curve with a relative minimum. The second derivative should exist at the minimum.</li>
			<li>What is the concavity?</li>
			</ol></p>
		</task>
	</activity>
</subsection>

</section>
